# Required Serialized Refs — use `this.Require`

Awake-boundary validation of required `[SerializeField]` references (see the non-null-parameter-contract rule) has exactly one implementation: the `RequiredRefs.Require` extension in the project's shared Unity core (`MonkeyTilt.Shared`, `Assets/_MonkeyTiltCore/Scripts/Runtime/Core/RequiredRefs.cs`).

```csharp
private void Awake()
{
    this.Require(_button, nameof(_button));
    this.Require(_label, nameof(_label));
}
```

It throws `MissingReferenceException` naming the component type, field, and object, and disables the component so a misconfigured scene/prefab fails loudly instead of half-running.

## Banned: hand-rolled validation blocks

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — hand-rolled copy of Require: softer (LogError instead of throw),
// doesn't name the field when several are checked, and duplicates the pattern
if (_button == null || _label == null)
{
    Debug.LogError("[MonkoButtonView] Awake — refs not assigned");
    enabled = false;
    return;
}
```

Projects enforce this mechanically with a convention guard (recompile + build preprocessor) that flags a null comparison followed shortly by `enabled = false` in runtime code.

## Allowed exceptions (marker: `// ref-guard: allow — reason`)

- **Lookup results, not serialized fields**: validating the result of `GetComponent*` / `Camera.main` / find-style resolution is a different boundary — `Require`'s "not wired" message would mislead. Keep the explicit check, add the marker.
- **Legitimately optional serialized fields** are not validated at all — they carry a comment at the field declaration and are checked at each feature entry point (see the non-null-parameter-contract rule), never with `enabled = false`.

